WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.450
The code is still here.

2
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:06.050
But the session that understood the release is gone.

3
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:07.810
What was finished?

4
00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:10.050
What is safe to start?

5
00:00:10.249 --> 00:00:13.010
And what does the next agent need to know?

6
00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:20.663
Agent Continuity keeps the project’s working state outside any one conversation.

7
00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:28.744
Agents and people can inspect it, update it, and hand work over without rebuilding the story from chat.

8
00:00:30.335 --> 00:00:35.055
Here, CedarPay is preparing a webhook reliability release.

9
00:00:35.314 --> 00:00:46.095
Retries must never duplicate an invoice, support needs replay visibility, and the rollout stays behind a canary until the evidence is strong enough.

10
00:00:47.681 --> 00:00:50.401
The board is not a flat to-do list.

11
00:00:50.561 --> 00:00:59.682
It already knows what is done, what is blocked, what is under review, and which two tasks can begin now because their dependencies are complete.

12
00:01:01.265 --> 00:01:04.385
Codex follows the installed skill first.

13
00:01:04.572 --> 00:01:12.466
It reads the project context, checks the task’s criteria and dependencies, and only then claims work.

14
00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:17.986
The skill guides the behavior; it does not schedule or dispatch the agent.

15
00:01:19.577 --> 00:01:23.657
At the same moment, Claude takes the operator-console task.

16
00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:31.737
The two agents claim different actionable tasks, with separate sessions and non-overlapping files.

17
00:01:31.881 --> 00:01:35.098
They share the project, not the same lease.

18
00:01:36.688 --> 00:01:40.688
A claim is a temporary lease, not a permanent assignment.

19
00:01:40.788 --> 00:01:47.249
Meaningful reports renew it, and another agent cannot simply force through a live claim.

20
00:01:47.468 --> 00:01:52.369
The shared board now shows exactly who is working, and where.

21
00:01:53.956 --> 00:02:03.957
Each agent records one useful milestone and one checkpoint: what is complete, what is happening now, and what comes next.

22
00:02:04.132 --> 00:02:09.797
This is durable resume state, not a transcript of every command.

23
00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.300
Test evidence is attached to the criterion it supports.

24
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:28.420
Agent Continuity stores the result and its provenance; it does not pretend the MCP call ran the test, and evidence alone does not mark the task complete.

25
00:02:30.006 --> 00:02:37.367
The signed retry bundle arrives from Test Operations, so its blocker is resolved with the checksum recorded.

26
00:02:37.586 --> 00:02:40.567
The task returns to ready.

27
00:02:40.646 --> 00:02:45.367
Nothing is auto-assigned, auto-run, or silently completed.

28
00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:50.694
The release also keeps its consequential choices with their reasoning.

29
00:02:50.841 --> 00:03:01.174
Using the provider event id protects redeliveries; treating terminal rows as no-op replays protects settled invoices.

30
00:03:01.374 --> 00:03:05.815
The rationale remains discoverable after the conversation ends.

31
00:03:07.388 --> 00:03:09.788
Now Codex can stop cleanly.

32
00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:17.869
The handoff validates its lease, writes a final checkpoint, creates the resume record, and releases the claim.

33
00:03:18.068 --> 00:03:22.989
The task is not declared done; its next action is explicit.

34
00:03:24.577 --> 00:03:38.018
Open the web UI and the same state is already there: Claude’s live execution, Codex’s checkpoint and handoff, the resolved blocker, decisions, and criterion evidence.

35
00:03:38.204 --> 00:03:43.217
Needs Attention makes the work requiring action easy to find.

36
00:03:44.796 --> 00:03:47.756
MCP is the primary agent interface.

37
00:03:47.936 --> 00:03:56.556
The CLI and web UI are complementary views over the same project state, not separate systems.

38
00:03:58.145 --> 00:04:07.105
Start Agent Continuity locally, connect an agent, and make your next multi-session project resumable by design.

